// The built-in types — DOCUMENTATION, NEVER COMPILED.
//
// `int32`, `string`, `isize` and the rest are registered in C++ (see `primKey` and `registerCollection`
// in src/kama.cemit.cpp); they are reserved words, not declarations, so this file is not — and cannot be
// — their definition. It exists so that the things which need a *place* for them have one: hover already
// answered for these names, and go-to-definition landed nowhere, which reads as the language server
// being broken rather than as the deliberate gap it is.
//
// This is the shape Go and Rust settled on for exactly this problem — Go's `builtin.go` declares `int`,
// `string`, `len` and `append` and is never compiled; Rust's `library/core/src/primitive_docs.rs` does
// the same. kama picks it over a synthesized virtual document because the prelude is already a real file
// that ships beside the stdlib, so this one rides the same road with no new machinery and no custom URI
// scheme for a client to honour.
//
// ⚠️ THIS FILE AND THE C++ REGISTRATIONS ARE TWO STATEMENTS OF ONE TRUTH, which is the failure mode a
// documentation-only file has. `tools/check-builtin-doc.sh` holds them together in BOTH directions —
// every name the compiler registers appears here, and every name here is one the compiler registers —
// because without that it rots exactly the way an unguarded prose claim does.
//
// ⚠️ THE LAYOUT IS LOAD-BEARING, not style. The compiler finds a name by scanning for its declaration
// line, so: one declaration per line, a type as `type <kind> <Name>`, a method as `fn <ret> <name>(`
// indented inside its type. Reformatting this file moves where go-to-definition lands.
//
// The bodies are empty on purpose. A primitive has no members to describe, and `string`'s methods are
// intrinsics whose real implementation is `kama_string__*` in include/kama_runtime.h — the authority for
// what each one does. What belongs here is the SIGNATURE, so a reader who jumped here learns the shape.

// ---- the scalar primitives ------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Fixed-width, all but the four whose width is the TARGET's (the two size types and C's `long` pair below),
// and that is the language's position rather than an omission: a width you can read is worth more than a
// width that follows the host. `int` is reserved and rejected with a message naming its replacements,
// because a C or Go reader will try it; so is `long`.

type value bool { }        // `true` / `false`

type value int8 { }        // signed, 8-bit
type value int16 { }       // signed, 16-bit
type value int32 { }       // signed, 32-bit — the default integer of the language
type value int64 { }       // signed, 64-bit

type value uint8 { }       // unsigned, 8-bit — also the element a `string` indexes to
type value uint16 { }      // unsigned, 16-bit
type value uint32 { }      // unsigned, 32-bit
type value uint64 { }      // unsigned, 64-bit

type value float32 { }     // IEEE-754 single
type value float64 { }     // IEEE-754 double

type value char { }        // one Unicode CODEPOINT, not a byte — `.chars()` yields these

// The two platform-width types, and the distinction between them is the one to get right: `isize` is a
// LENGTH or an offset (it is what `string.length()` returns and what indexing takes), `usize` is the C
// ABI's size type and belongs where a value crosses into C — `sizeof`, an allocation, an `extern fn`.
type value isize { }       // signed platform width   (C `ptrdiff_t`)
type value usize { }       // unsigned platform width (C `size_t`)

// The C ABI elements, for an `extern` that must agree with a C prototype byte for byte. `clong`/`culong`
// are C's `long`/`unsigned long` — 4 bytes on Windows, 8 elsewhere, so `isize` would be WRONG there — and
// take the same refusals as the size types (`sizeof` does not fold, a literal's range is not checked, no
// wire format). `cchar` is C's `char`: NOT a value — kama's `char` is a 32-bit codepoint and C's is a byte
// that is a third type beside `int8`/`uint8` — but the element `UnsafePtr<cchar>`/`UnsafeConstPtr<cchar>`
// point at, which is what `string.cstr()` returns and what a `const char*` parameter is spelled as. Any
// bare `cchar` (a local, a field, a generic argument, `p[i]`, `sizeof`) is refused; the bytes are a
// `cast<UnsafePtr<uint8>>` away.
type value cchar { }       // C `char`, as a raw-pointer element only
type value clong { }       // C `long`          (target width)
type value culong { }      // C `unsigned long` (target width)

type value void { }        // no value — a return type only, never a variable's

// ---- string ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// IMMUTABLE and owned. Every method below reads; the ones that appear to change a string (`concat`,
// `trim`, `substring`, the case pair) return a NEW owned string and leave the receiver untouched, which
// is why each is safe to call on a borrowed one.
//
// It indexes to BYTES — `s[i]` is a `uint8` — and iterates codepoints only through `.chars()`. That
// split is deliberate and is the one thing to know before writing string code: a byte offset is not a
// character offset, `substring` traps on an offset that splits a character, and `floorCharBoundary` is
// the total function that snaps an arbitrary offset down to a safe one.
type value string {
    public fn isize length();                                   // length in BYTES, not codepoints
    public fn bool equals(string other);                        // also what `==` resolves to
    public fn string concat(string other);                      // a new owned string
    public fn UnsafeConstPtr<cchar> cstr();                     // FFI: the NUL-terminated C pointer (`const char*`), read-only
    public fn uint8 get(isize index);                           // `s[i]` — the i-th BYTE
    public fn Chars chars();                                    // codepoint iterator (see the prelude)

    public fn string substring(isize start, isize end);         // traps on a non-boundary offset
    public fn isize floorCharBoundary(isize at);                // snap DOWN to a safe offset, total, O(1)
    public fn string truncate(isize maxBytes);                  // substring on a floored budget

    public fn bool contains(string substring);
    public fn bool startsWith(string prefix);
    public fn bool endsWith(string suffix);
    public fn bool isEmpty();
    public fn Optional<usize> find(string substring);           // None when it is not there

    public fn string trim();
    public fn string trimStart();
    public fn string trimEnd();
    public fn string replace(string old, string with);          // `with:` — `new` is a keyword
    public fn string toLower();
    public fn string toUpper();
    public fn Split split(string separator);                    // lazy — pieces come out one at a time
}

// ---- the intrinsic generic types --------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Everything else that once lived here is an ordinary library type now: `DynamicArray`, `FixedArray`,
// `View` and `Map` are `std::collections`, and `Owned`/`Shared`/`Weak` are `std::memory`. Go-to-definition
// on those already opens their real sources. These three stay intrinsic because each needs something the
// language cannot yet say about itself.

// A raw C pointer, and the whole of kama's unsafe boundary. Bare `UnsafePtr` is C's `void*`; storing one
// is safe, and only DEREFERENCING it needs `unsafe`.
type value UnsafePtr<T> { }

// The read-only raw pointer: C's `const T*` (bare, `const void*`). Reading through it is the same `unsafe`
// as `UnsafePtr`; STORING through it is a compile error, and it does not convert to `UnsafePtr<T>` (the
// reverse conversion is implicit, as in C). It is what a `const fn` hands out — `dataPtr()`, `cstr()` —
// so a buffer held `const` can reach C without laundering deep const: Rust's `*const T`, Swift's
// `UnsafePointer<T>`, kama's `get`/`getRef` split applied at the FFI seam.
type value UnsafeConstPtr<T> { }

// The const-generic value array: `struct { T v[N]; }`, sized at compile time. It owns no heap, copies by
// value and has no destructor, which is what makes it — unlike every other container — a `value`.
type value InlineArray<T> comptime(int32 N) { }

// A callable with its receiver bound in. The type argument is a function SIGNATURE rather than a type,
// which is why it is not an ordinary generic.
type value BindableFunctionPtr<Sig> { }

// A LANE BATCH: N numbers the CPU operates on as one value. `Simd<float32>#(4)` is 16 bytes, and `a + b`
// on two of them is a single machine instruction.
//
// It is not an `InlineArray`, and the distinction is what each is FOR. An `InlineArray` is a container —
// you index it, iterate it, and its lanes mean whatever you decide. A `Simd`'s lanes are interchangeable
// and it has no `foreach` and no `[i]`, because taking a lane batch apart one lane at a time is exactly
// what it exists to avoid. `std::math`'s `Vec4` is a third thing again: geometry, whose lanes are named
// `x/y/z/w` and mean different things.
//
// Reach for it only for what the compiler's auto-vectorizer cannot express on its own — an arbitrary
// shuffle, a two-vector blend, a lane mask as a value. An ordinary loop over `Vec4`s already compiles to
// SIMD on every target kama ships, and often *better* than a hand-written lane batch would.
//
// `sizeof(T) * N` must be 16: 128 bits is the only width every kama target has. The element must be a
// numeric primitive. A target with no vector unit is not an error — the operations lower to correct
// scalar code, which is why this is a type rather than a bag of per-CPU intrinsics.
type value Simd<T> comptime(int32 N) { }
